<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFrameworks>net8.0-windows;net9.0-windows;net10.0-windows</TargetFrameworks>
    <UseWPF>true</UseWPF>
    <GenerateAssemblyInfo>true</GenerateAssemblyInfo>
    <PackageId>Trustsoft.NotifyIcon</PackageId>
    <RootNamespace>Trustsoft.NotifyIcon</RootNamespace>
    <AssemblyName>Trustsoft.NotifyIcon</AssemblyName>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    Package metadata (R010). These values live in the library project rather than in
    Directory.Build.props because this is the only project in the repository that is ever packed:
    a repository-wide property group for a population of one would only obscure which project the
    settings belong to, and it would leak "PackageId Trustsoft.NotifyIcon" onto the sample and the
    tests, which must never be packable at all (see Non-shipping-projects note below).
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <IsPackable>true</IsPackable>
    <!--
      The tray-only pre-release of the v1 surface. SemVer with breaking changes only in major
      versions is D010; the version policy and what would move the number are D037; the decision to
      ship M001's tray surface ahead of M002's toasts as a documented pre-release, keeping 1.0.0
      reserved for the release that carries both, is D072. This number, 1.0.0-preview.2, exists
      because adding the source remote made a sentence in this package's own README false - it said
      the repository had no remote - and a shipped document has to tell the truth about the
      repository a consumer got the package from; preview.1 keeps its number and its pushed tag
      instead of being reissued with different content under the same number (D074). A prerelease suffix is a release act a
      consumer can see - NuGet will not resolve it for a plain install, and a public feed never
      frees the number - so it is a deliberate edit here, in the README install line (guarded by
      Readme_documents_the_install_line_usage_and_the_shipped_surface) and in
      PackagePurityTests.ExpectedPackageVersion, and nowhere else. `Version` also drives
      AssemblyVersion/FileVersion/AssemblyInformationalVersion, so there is no second place to bump.
    -->
    <Version>1.0.0-preview.2</Version>
    <Authors>Trustsoft</Authors>
    <Description>A notification-area (system tray) icon for WPF applications that need no window and no System.Windows.Forms. Registers the icon through the library's own Shell_NotifyIcon P/Invoke declarations, so the shipped assembly carries no package reference beyond the platform: no WinForms, no System.Drawing.Common and no tray-helper package. Targets net8.0-windows, net9.0-windows and net10.0-windows.</Description>
    <PackageTags>wpf;tray;tray-icon;notification-area;notifyicon;system-tray;taskbar;no-winforms;p-invoke</PackageTags>
    <PackageLicenseExpression>MIT</PackageLicenseExpression>
    <PackageReadmeFile>README.md</PackageReadmeFile>
    <!--
      PackageOutputPath is set here, not repository-wide, for the same reason as the rest of the
      metadata: exactly one project is packable. The path is written relative to this project file
      (`src/Trustsoft.NotifyIcon/`), so the nupkg lands in the repository's own `artifacts/`
      directory, which is gitignored - a packed artifact is never a source file.
    -->
    <PackageOutputPath>$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)..\..\artifacts\</PackageOutputPath>
    <!--
      R011 / "no dependency group beyond WPF" is asserted as "no <dependency> entry in any group",
      not as "no <dependencies> element" (D038): the SDK emits one EMPTY <group targetFramework=...>
      per lib folder, and that is not a dependency claim - it is how the nuspec declares which
      frameworks the package has a lib for, and the SDK's own validation enforces the pairing. Measured: setting
      SuppressDependenciesWhenPacking=true removes the element and the pack then fails with
      NU5128 ("add a dependency group for net8.0-windows7.0"), which TreatWarningsAsErrors turns
      into an error. The empty groups therefore stay, and the guard lives in the test suite
      (PackagePurityTests) and in scripts/verify-package.sh.
    -->
    <!--
      Deliberately absent: RepositoryUrl and PackageProjectUrl. The source remote is a git host,
      not a package feed, and no public project page exists; a URL in package metadata cannot be
      corrected after publication, so the omission stays deliberate and asserted.
      therefore no real URL to point at, and a fabricated link in package metadata is worse than an
      absent one - it sends a consumer to a page that does not exist and it cannot be corrected
      after publication. Both are omitted on purpose; PackagePurityTests asserts their absence so
      that adding one later is a deliberate edit against a URL that actually resolves.
    -->
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    The README and the licence travel inside the nupkg (R010): PackageReadmeFile names README.md,
    so the file has to be an actual package entry at the package root, and the MIT licence text is
    shipped alongside the SPDX expression rather than only referenced by it.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <None Include="$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)..\..\README.md" Pack="true" PackagePath="\" Visible="false" />
    <None Include="$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)..\..\LICENSE" Pack="true" PackagePath="\" Visible="false" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    Zero PackageReference entries by design (R011, D001): the library talks to the shell
    through its own P/Invoke declarations. No System.Drawing.Common (removed from the
    shared framework in .NET 8+ and would add a dependency for HICON ownership we already
    handle ourselves) and no Microsoft.Windows.Compatibility.
  -->

  <!--
    Non-shipping projects. The sample and the test project declare IsPackable=false in their own
    project files, and scripts/probe-live carries the same property as defence in depth. The probe
    cannot reach the package by construction as well as by property: it is absent from
    Trustsoft.NotifyIcon.sln (so `dotnet pack Trustsoft.NotifyIcon.sln` cannot see it) and it takes
    no project reference to the library, which is what keeps its presence oracle independent of the
    code it checks (S06 hand-off). PackagePurityTests pins all of this.
  -->

</Project>
